Give the engine back its memory pressure response.

There is no swap on this system, so memory WebKit holds is memory nothing
else can have, and the engine has to hand it back before the machine is
full.  It never did.  Upstream has two mechanisms for that, and the platform
selection compiled both out here, silently:

  1. The per-process monitor.  MemoryPressureHandler measures the process
     every 30 s through memoryFootprint() and, above a third of
     min(3 GB, RAM), prunes caches and curbs the JavaScript heap; above a
     half it drops everything.  On this system memoryFootprint() was
     generic/MemoryFootprintGeneric.cpp, which returns 0 with system malloc,
     so the policy stayed Unrestricted forever -- and the web process never
     switched the monitor on anyway, because WebProcess.cpp does so only
     with FastMalloc ("some kind of testing or debugging is probably
     happening" otherwise) and the port builds with USE_SYSTEM_MALLOC=ON.

  2. The system monitor.  On Linux the UI process watches /proc/meminfo
     (UIProcess/linux/MemoryPressureMonitor.cpp) and, at 90% of RAM in use,
     tells every process over IPC to release memory; at 95%, to release it
     all.  Monitor, message and receiver are all #if OS(LINUX).

The system monitor is the one that matters without swap, and it cannot
simply be switched on: the message is declared under OS(LINUX) in
AuxiliaryProcess.messages.in, its receiver in AuxiliaryProcess.h, and the
handler's trigger for it in wtf/MemoryPressureHandler.h -- three headers
that between them are included by most of the engine, so widening any of
them rebuilds it all.

So the monitor moves into the handler.  wtf/likeos/MemoryPressureHandlerLikeOS.cpp
is this system's platform file, replacing the generic one: every process
installs the handler already (WebProcess, NetworkProcess, GPUProcess, and the
UI process through installMemoryPressureHandler()), and install() there
starts one thread per process that reads /proc/meminfo the way the Linux
monitor does, on its polling curve, and delivers the verdict to its own
process instead of over IPC.  Same knob, WEBKIT_DISABLE_MEMORY_PRESSURE_MONITOR=1.
The response and hold-off are those of unix/MemoryPressureHandlerUnix.cpp,
with the hold-off timer -- a member declared under the same OS list --
replaced by a deadline the thread honours.

The thresholds are lower than Linux's, and the reason is the swap.  Linux
declares pressure at 90% and critical at 95%: the last 10%, plus swap, is
what covers the time between the machine getting there and the processes
responding.  Here there is no plus.  The reserve alone has to cover one
polling interval of allocation -- a page load can take a hundred megabytes
in the second between polls -- and the response itself, which allocates
before it frees.  Doubling the reserve is the adjustment: pressure at 80%,
critical at 90%, the polling curve shifted down the same ten points
(one-second polls from 75% in use, five-second ones below 40%).  "In use" is
MemTotal minus MemAvailable, and the kernel now counts its page cache as
available, since it drops that on demand.

memoryFootprint() becomes linux/MemoryFootprintLinux.cpp, reading
/proc/self/statm, which the kernel gained for this; and the web process
switches the periodic monitor on with system malloc, which on this system is
the only malloc, not a debugging choice.  With a real footprint the policy
thresholds start to mean something: on a 4 GB machine a web process is
Conservative from 1 GB resident and Strict from 1.5 GB, and an inactive one
that reaches 0.9 x RAM is killed rather than allowed to take the machine
with it.

The UI process installs the handler on this system as it does on Linux; the
guard there also gated it on the Linux monitor's own disable check, which
the handler now performs itself.

